# 0069-open-in-ibuilder.patch -- "Open in iBuilder": a game file handed over by the Files app's Share sheet (or any app's Open In) is added WITHOUT a picker and without a copy: the original arrives as a security-scoped URL
# and is cloned into Documents (patch 0066's GameImport.Place); a copy iOS made in this app's own container is moved (a rename).
# Applies after 0068 (the tree with 0010..0068 applied). UNTESTED ON DEVICE: compiled (dotnet build, ios-arm64, Release: rc 0, no error, the same warnings as the tree without it: this patch adds none; the final count is in the build log) and the pure classes
# run in a host harness (a net console program built from the real source text of GameAppOpenIn.cs and the files it uses: 262 checks, including the REAL clonefile and a REAL rename on this Mac's APFS, the plist transform on the
# REAL Info.plist.fragment and on the Info.plist the real build produced, Apple's plutil as the independent reader; 48 mutations of the classes and of the wiring's text, each caught); no iPhone run. Measured and assumed are told apart below.
#
# WHY (field facts on the owner's iPhone 15 Pro, iOS 27.2.1, app installed through SideStore): NO Open-mode document picker delivers its selection any more: "Add from Files without copying" logs `import: the picker is open (in
#   place)` and then nothing, and the folder pickers (Settings > Cloud > Add a folder, for iCloud Drive and for On My iPhone > Downloads) do nothing on Open; only the Import-mode picker works (it copies the whole 11-26 GB file:
#   minutes). The simulator (iOS 27.2) works, so it is a real-device issue. This patch is a route that does not depend on a picker at all: in Files, touch and hold the game, Share, iBuilder. When the app declares document types
#   for the game containers and LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace, iOS hands the app the ORIGINAL file in place; the app clones it into Documents (instant, no extra space), or copies it when a clone is refused.
#
# WHAT IT DOES
#   * Info.plist.fragment: UTImportedTypeDeclarations (dev.nxpocket.ibuilder.nsp / .xci / .nsz / .nup: public.filename-extension, conforming to public.data) and ONE CFBundleDocumentTypes entry ("Switch game", role Viewer, LSHandlerRank
#       Alternate: iBuilder is offered, never made the default of anybody's files) whose LSItemContentTypes are those four. UIFileSharingEnabled and LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace (both true, the keys verify_ipa.sh looks
#       for) were already there and are unchanged. Nothing else is touched. prod.keys is NOT a document type: only the four game extensions.
#   * GameAppMain.cs (small): GameAppAppDelegate gets `application:openURL:options:` (a scene-less delivery: iOS does not use it for a scene app, it is the safety net) and GameAppSceneDelegate gets `scene:openURLContexts:` (the
#       app is running or suspended) and, in WillConnect, reads options.UrlContexts (a COLD start through Open in delivers the URL there) after the window is key and visible. All three only queue the URLs (a few property
#       reads); nothing else runs in the callbacks.
#   * GameAppOpenIn.cs (new; no UIKit, run by the harness): OpenIn (which names are game files: .nsp .xci .nsz .nup in any case, never prod.keys, InPlaceImport.SafeName's rules; the plan of a batch with the log line of every
#       skipped file; the ROUTE of a file: AlreadyThere / MoveFromInbox / InPlace, decided from the path with /private and ".." resolved and whole folders compared: only a file inside THIS app's Documents/Inbox, tmp/ or temp folder is
#       ever moved, anything else is somebody's original and is only read; the move delegate; the words), OpenInQueue (FIFO, thread-safe: the same file twice is queued once, a delivery while a batch runs waits behind it), OpenInPlist
#       (the transform of the exported player's Info.plist).
#   * GameAppOpenInUi.cs (new): GameAppOpenInEntry (the three doors into one queue, plus a note list for what arrives before the library exists), and GameAppViewController's pump and worker. The pump (main thread, property
#       reads only): nothing queued -> nothing; a player -> every URL is refused in the log; a batch running -> it waits (its end pumps again); another import (a picker's) running -> a 1 s look, one log line `open-in: waiting for
#       the import that is running`; else everything that waits is ONE batch: `open-in: received <name> (in place: yes/no)` for each, `open-in: not a game file: <name>` for the others, then the worker. The worker (Task.Run) is 0066's
#       "Add from Files without copying" with the same card (`import-progress`), the same awake-screen rule, a UIApplication background task whose handler ends the TASK, and per file: IN PLACE (iOS's original or any URL not in
#       our container): StartAccessingSecurityScopedResource (stopped in a finally), an NSFileCoordinator read (a file only in iCloud is downloaded first), GameImport.Place into Documents/<the file's own name> (the other import
#       paths' naming; update, DLC and the title id are read from the name by the refresh that follows), `import: cloned ...` / `import: copied ...` (0066's lines); the original is never changed or deleted. NOT in place (iOS copied
#       it into Documents/Inbox or tmp/): a MOVE (a rename onto <name>.part, then the one rename over the old file), `import: moved <name> (<size>) in <ms> ms (...)`, no coordination, no copy, no space; if the rename is refused
#       Place copies and the Inbox copy is deleted after it. The Build tab is shown only when the library is on screen. When the batch ends: the summary line (`open-in: imported: A.nsp (cloned), B.nsp (moved) ...`), RefreshGames,
#       RefreshScreen, 0066's "Not all were added" alert for failures, and the next batch if one arrived meanwhile. A set of URLs has no order: the files of one delivery are taken by name.
#   * GameAppShelf.cs (2 lines): the + button's menu is ImportMenuWithOpenIn(): 0066's two entries unchanged, then, apart, a row "How to add from Files" (alert with the steps and an "Open Files" button); the Build tab's empty
#       state gets a second muted line with the hint.
#   * GameAppExport.cs (1 line): InfoPlist() of an export ends with OpenInPlist.ForPlayerOrAsIs(<the XML NSPropertyListSerialization wrote>): the player drops CFBundleDocumentTypes and the four game types of
#       UTImportedTypeDeclarations (another imported type would stay, and the key with it) and keeps everything else byte for byte: UTExportedTypeDeclarations (its .romfs type), UIFileSharingEnabled, the scene manifest...
#       If the plist could not be read as XML (not expected) it is exported as it is and `[gameapp] open-in: the player's Info.plist keeps its document types: ...` goes to stderr: an export is not refused for it.
#
# DECISION THAT DIFFERS FROM THE BRIEF: LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace is KEPT in the player's Info.plist (the brief said to strip it). Together with UIFileSharingEnabled it is what shows an app's Documents folder in the
#   Files app (the fragment's own comment and GameAppMain.cs's header say so: "the folder invisible over Files" was the first device run's stop), and the exported player's first screen is "Open Files" on its own folder (patch
#   0060: the copy made in Files is the only route that works in a FlareStore-installed player). Without CFBundleDocumentTypes the key makes the player an "Open in" target for nothing. OpenInPlist.ForPlayer(xml,
#   dropInPlaceKey: true) removes it too: one argument if a device shows the player does not need it.
#
# NEW STRINGS: "How to add from Files" (+ menu row; subtitle "Touch and hold the game, Share, iBuilder"), alert "Add from Files": "In Files, touch and hold the game, tap Share, then iBuilder. The game is added where it is,
#   without a copy.\n\nSeveral files (a game with its update and DLC) can be shared together." with the buttons "Open Files" and "Close"; the Build tab's hint "In Files, touch and hold the game, Share, iBuilder: it is added
#   without a copy."; reused from 0066: "Adding", "Copying", "Not all were added", "OK". Log lines: `open-in: <door> delivered <N> URL(s)` (door = scene:willConnectToSession, scene:openURLContexts, application:openURL:options:),
#   `open-in: received <name> (in place: yes|no)`, `open-in: not a game file: <name>`, `open-in: <name> skipped: <why>`, `open-in: <name> is already waiting or being added: ignored`, `open-in: waiting for the import that is
#   running`, `import: moved <name> (<size>) in <ms> ms (...)`, 0066's `import: cloned ...` / `import: copied ...`, `open-in: <name> could not be added: <why>`, `open-in: imported: ... (cloned|moved|copied|already there)`.
#   ACCESSIBILITY IDS: import-howto (the menu row), open-in-hint (the empty-state line); import-progress (0066's card) is shared; import-file is the + button, as before.
#
# MEASURED (host harness, .NET 11, Apple silicon Mac, APFS; 262 checks): the names (the four extensions in any case, prod.keys / .part / .icloud / a folder / a name too long for the .part refused; a batch planned in order with an iCloud
#   placeholder's real name, a name twice skipped with its line); the route over twenty-odd path shapes (iOS's copy in Documents/Inbox or tmp/ also spelled under /private/var is MoveFromInbox; Inbox-old, Inboxes, tmpfile, a ".." that leaves Inbox,
#   a folder named Inbox in iCloud Drive, another app's Documents are InPlace; the library's own file is AlreadyThere; a temp folder of "/" is refused); the queue (FIFO, the same file twice once, a delivery during a batch waits behind it,
#   8 threads x 500 files delivered twice each: every file arrived, none twice in one batch); the REAL clonefile through GameImport.Place: a 200 MB original in a "file provider" folder is cloned into a fake container's Documents in 1.1 ms,
#   free space change 0.0 MB, SHA-256 identical, the original untouched (content and time), writing into the clone leaves the original as it was; iOS's copy of 300 MB in Documents/Inbox is MOVED in 0.42 ms, the SAME inode (a rename),
#   free space change 0.0 MB, the Inbox neighbour untouched; a refused rename falls back to the copy and says why; a rename and a copy that both fail leave the old game and iOS's copy; a copy that cannot fit is refused up front and a
#   rename needs no room; a file that is gone is reported and nothing is created. The plist: the REAL fragment is valid for plutil and xmllint (the strict check build-app.sh runs), plutil (Apple's reader) agrees on the document type
#   (one entry, Alternate, Viewer, the four UTIs) and on the four imported types (public.data, public.filename-extension nsp xci nsz nup); the player's transform leaves NO CFBundleDocumentTypes and NO UTImportedTypeDeclarations, every
#   other top-level key identical in order and in value (read by plutil), UIFileSharingEnabled / LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace / its .romfs type kept, idempotent, a foreign imported type kept with its key, a nested key of the same
#   name untouched, 20,000 keys in a few ms, garbage refused (FormatException; ForPlayerOrAsIs returns it as it is and says so), an external entity never read; the same transform on the Info.plist of the REAL build (the SDK merged the
#   fragment: the built binary plist has the document type and the four types) passes plutil -lint with every other key identical. The UIKit files are read as text for what the host cannot run: the three doors and the cold start, the
#   export going through the transform, no file work or wait on the main thread in any callback or in the pump, every file operation inside the worker, the security scope stopped in a finally, the background task ended, the only
#   File.Delete is of iOS's own copy, the + menu row added to the menu the button has (the pickers' file is not touched). 48 mutations (the classes, the fragment and that wiring text), each caught by at least one check.
#   COMPILED: dotnet build gameapp.csproj -c Release -r ios-arm64 on a copy of the main agent's proj folder with the patched sources (paths rewritten, Compile list regenerated, clean obj/bin): rc 0, 0 errors, 47 warnings, the same
#   warning set as the tree without this patch (the normalised lists are identical); the same on top of the other agent's 0067 draft (rc 0); this patch applies on that draft without a rejected hunk. In the built GameApp.dll: the UTF-16
#   strings "In Files, touch and hold the game, Share, iBuilder: it is added without a copy.", "How to add from Files", "open-in: received ", "open-in: not a game file: ", "import: moved ", and the metadata names
#   application:openURL:options:, scene:openURLContexts:, GameAppOpenInEntry, OpenInPlist (candidates for verify_ipa.sh).
# NOT EXERCISED / ASSUMED (no device run, no simulator run): everything UIKit (the callbacks, the pump, the card, the menu row, the alert, the hint, the timer); that iOS offers iBuilder in the Files app's Share sheet for a .nsp / .xci / .nsz
#   / .nup (the types are declared as IMPORTED ones with the app's own identifiers: if another installed app exports a type for the same extension with another identifier, Files may resolve the file to that one and not list this
#   app); the registration of the new types by an installer (SideStore) at install; that the delivery is in place (the flag is read and logged: `(in place: yes/no)`) and the URL is the original; the cold start through Open in (options.
#   UrlContexts); that the sandbox lets clonefile read a security-scoped original on the phone (a refusal is the copy, and the line says why); where iOS puts a copy that is not in place (Documents/Inbox or tmp/ is assumed from Apple's
#   documentation; any other place is read as an original: cloned or copied, never moved or deleted); a 26 GB file; the progress card while the app is in the background; that the player's Documents folder stays visible in Files with
#   the key kept (assumed from the repo's own record of the first device run); a delivery while a game is on screen.
# RISKS: iBuilder now appears in the Share sheet / Open In list of any app for .nsp, .xci, .nsz and .nup files (rank Alternate: never the default; only those four extensions; the exported players do not); the types are declared with the
#   app's own identifiers, so another app that declares the same extensions competes for the file's type; a SideStore-installed app has a team-suffixed bundle id, which does not touch the type identifiers; a URL set from a cold start has
#   no order, so the files of one delivery are taken by name; a file the person shares twice while it is being added is dropped (the log says so), shared again afterwards it is replaced in one rename; an Inbox copy is renamed away,
#   so Files' Inbox folder of this app is empty after an add; each file added here IS recorded in the 0068 ledger (gameapp.imports.tsv, source "Open in iBuilder", how clone or copy), so with Settings > "Remove the game file after a successful build" on, it is replaced by a
#   zero-byte stub after its successful build like any other import (the original in Files is never touched; a file that was already the library's own is never recorded); with 0067 in the tree the in-place branch of OpenInRun could call 0067's RunImportJobs instead of its own loop (it has no per-URL security scope there: the scope of each URL would have to be started
#   by the caller).
